  <!-- FAQ SECTION SLOT -->
  <Fragment slot="faq">
    <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800" open>
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            1. What is exponential form?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Exponential form represents repeated multiplication of a number or prime factor in a compact format using a base and a power (superscript exponent), such as 128 = 2⁷.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            2. How do I write a whole number in exponential form?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Determine the prime factorization of the positive whole number, group identical prime factors together, count how many times each prime factor appears, and write each factor with its frequency as an exponent.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            3. What is the exponential form of 128?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          The prime factorization of 128 is 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2, which simplifies in exponential form to 2⁷.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            4. How do I convert logarithmic form to exponential form?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Use the equivalence log_b(c) = a ⟺ bᵃ = c. The base of the logarithm becomes the base of the power, the logarithm's value becomes the exponent, and the argument becomes the result.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            5. How do I convert exponential form to logarithmic form?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Apply the reverse relationship bᵃ = c ⟺ log_b(c) = a. The base remains the base of the logarithm, the power's result becomes the logarithm argument, and the exponent becomes the isolated value.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            6. What is the relationship between logarithms and exponents?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Logarithms and exponentiation are inverse operations. While exponentiation computes the result of raising a base to a power (e.g. 2³ = 8), a logarithm determines what power is needed to produce a given number (e.g. log₂(8) = 3).
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            7. Can decimals be written in exponential form?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Decimals cannot be factored into prime numbers. Instead, non-integers and decimals are expressed using scientific notation (or exponential notation) as a mantissa multiplied by a power of 10, such as 24.65 = 2.465 × 10¹.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            8. What restrictions apply to a logarithm's base?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          The base b must satisfy b &gt; 0 and b ≠ 1. A base of 1 is invalid because 1 raised to any power remains 1, and negative or zero bases lead to undefined or non-real results.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            9. Can I use e as a logarithm base?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Yes. When the base is Euler's mathematical constant e (approximately 2.71828), the logarithm is called the natural logarithm, denoted as ln(c) = a, which is equivalent to eᵃ = c.
        </p>
      </details>

